<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thaya</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dyje</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Thaya (Czech Dyje) is a river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>, about 235 kilometres long. Rising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-austria">Lower Austria</a>, it flows through southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/znojmo">Znojmo</a> and for long stretches forms the Austrian-Czech border. It joins the Morava near the meeting point of Austria, the Czech Republic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thaya.jpg" alt="Thaya" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fredericus · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:B%C5%99eclav,_Dyje.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Thaya (Czech Dyje) is a right tributary of the Morava River. It is formed by the confluence of the German Thaya and the Moravian Thaya at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raba">Raabs</a> an der Thaya, and flows through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-austria">Lower Austria</a> and the South Moravian Region. Together with the German Thaya, its main source, it is 311 km long; counted from the confluence, it is 235.4 km. The German Thaya rises in the territory of Schweiggers at 658 m, and the river enters the Morava at Lanžhot at 151 m, the southernmost and lowest point of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>. The drainage basin covers 13,419 square kilometres and the river has 573 tributaries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Both names, Thaya and Dyje, come from the Illyrian word duja, which can be translated as rushing river. The first written mention is from 985, when the name was written Taja. Part of the course forms the Austrian-Czech border. The most populated settlements on the river are the towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/znojmo">Znojmo</a> and Břeclav. For water management the Thaya and the German Thaya count as two different rivers with separate numbering of river kilometres. The largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a>, the three Nové Mlýny reservoirs, are built directly on the river, with others at Znojmo and Vranov.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The river flows through the Podyjí and Thayatal national parks, where protected species include the European crayfish and the brown trout, and the common kingfisher nests along it. The confluence area with the Morava, with its pools, oxbow lakes and channels, is the most valuable ichthyological site in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>: about 80 per cent of native Czech fish species live there, 15 per cent of them found only there. Since 2024 it has been protected as the Soutok protected landscape area. About 209 km of the river is navigable, and it passes through the Lednice–Valtice Cultural Landscape, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thaya” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The princes ruled from the castle of Znojmo on a crag above the <b>Dyje</b> (Thaya) river, close to the Austrian border.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-znojmo">Duchy of Znojmo</a></li></ul></section>
